WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.143 --> 00:00:02.560
- A spacesuit has to do three things.

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:03.860
It has to maintain pressure

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:06.660
so your blood doesn't start
boiling in the vacuum of space.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.260
It has to provide oxygen
so you can breathe.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:11.080
And it has to protect
against the environment:

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:14.280
extreme heat, extreme cold,
and direct sunlight.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.250
This...

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:18.040
...won't do any of those.
It's just a replica of that one.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.857
That's the actual Sokol spacesuit

00:00:19.857 --> 00:00:22.850
that Tim Peake, the most recent
British astronaut, used in space.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:24.330
The folks here at the
National Space Centre

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:27.070
wouldn't let me try on a real one,
which is fair enough.

00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:30.270
Versions of that suit have been protecting
astronauts since the 1970s.

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:31.910
And there are variants still used today.

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:33.950
It's not designed for
hours-long space walks.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.900
It's basically an
emergency personal lifeboat

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:40.180
for if the ship you're travelling on...
uh, springs a leak.

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:41.780
And it provides those three things:

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.920
pressure, oxygen,
environmental protection.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:47.320
Now a couple of months ago,
I had a video where an AI

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.250
tried to suggest stories and
titles that I could cover.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:51.340
And one of the things it came up with

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:55.040
was "the long-forgotten history
of the British moon landings".

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.560
Not strictly true, of course.
We never actually got there.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.930
But one of the team here got in touch,

00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:02.110
and it turns out that in the 1940s,

00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:06.080
15 years before Kennedy said that
America "chose to go to the moon",

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.320
the British Interplanetary Society
did have similar plans,

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.800
including our own spacesuit.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.980
- The BIS was set up in 1933
by a man called Phil Cleator.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:18.400
And he had this plan.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.770
He wanted to get together
with like-minded individuals

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:22.520
who were interested in space flight,

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:25.400
and what the possible future
of space flight might be.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.970
Rocket technology was suddenly
moving forward in a way

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:31.390
that, actually, spaceflight
was something tangible

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:33.310
and might well be realistic.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:35.370
- The BIS weren't seriously expecting

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:37.700
to launch a mission to
the moon on their own.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:38.847
But they did want to say,

00:01:38.847 --> 00:01:42.930
"Hey, we think this is possible.
And we think Britain should do it."

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:44.950
And one of their number,
illustrator Ralph Smith,

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:47.370
drew up plans for a spacesuit.

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:49.720
- So we have an exhibition here
at the National Space Centre

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:51.190
that focuses on the space race.

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:54.060
And people tend to think about
America and the Soviet Union.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.390
And we wanted to tell something

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:57.990
of what Britain was up to
at the same time.

00:01:57.990 --> 00:01:59.810
As we were planning for our exhibition,

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:01.750
we were leafing through
a research document

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:03.890
and looking at the history of the BIS.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:05.660
And immediately this picture jumped out

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:10.213
of somebody wearing this incredible-
looking spacesuit that they'd designed.

00:02:10.213 --> 00:02:12.970
And immediately we just said,
"We have to make this."

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:16.390
- It looks wonderful,
but no one ever built it...

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:18.090
until a few years ago.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:20.610
- So, I get a call,
and where do you start?

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:23.527
You've got two drawings basically.
Rather than just say,

00:02:23.527 --> 00:02:26.620
"Well, I'll make a replica,
a facsimile of it."

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:30.200
I thought "Let's look at the materials
that were available at the time."

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:33.410
Cotton and rubber, much
like a modern space suit

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:37.617
is made out of beta fabric and
a rubber pressure layer inside.

00:02:37.617 --> 00:02:39.460
And the pressure's inside it.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:43.260
It can't expand out any further.
It can't balloon out.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:45.960
He would have an oxygen
supply in his backpack there.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.830
So he's being fed this oxygen supply

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:51.950
going through, past his
face, down through the suit,

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:54.700
and recycling back into the backpack again

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.770
for expulsion in the way
just like an Apollo suit did.

00:02:57.770 --> 00:02:59.830
Here you've got
what we call the "convolutes".

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:02.950
And they're to give a bit
of movement at the arm.

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:06.120
And right up here, you've
got cloth of silver.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:09.280
If he's getting cold on
the surface of the moon,

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:12.370
he can then pull this cape around him.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:15.970
And now any heat that's
radiating out from the suit,

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:17.960
he can then trap that heat in.

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:20.150
And it reflects the heat
back into the body again.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:22.680
So these little pins
here, they are, in fact

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.000
little stand-off mushrooms.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.930
So when the cape is hanging over them,

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:30.100
there's a gap between
the surface of the suit

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:31.350
and the surfaces of the cape.

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:33.380
And that gap of vacuum

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:36.860
means there's no physical
thermal transfer from the suit

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:38.490
out into the vacuum of space.

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:40.510
You'll have noticed that
on the front of his chest,

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:42.570
he's got this rather strange device here.

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:43.760
This is an airlock.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:46.200
Imagine the circumstances.
You're on the surface of the moon.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.060
You find an interesting rock.
You open the airlock.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:52.300
You put the rock into the air lock.
You close the airlock.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:55.490
Now you withdraw your arms inside the suit

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:58.310
through these exceptionally
large holes here.

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:01.810
And using that long front,
you can examine that rock

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:04.540
that you've just taken
off the lunar surface.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:06.880
But you also notice,
there is a shooting stick.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:09.420
And it gives you a walking stick-cum-seat.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.350
You get tired on the surface of the moon.

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:15.030
You open the seat part of the
shooting stick and you sit down.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:17.350
They've kind of thought
of everything, really.

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:22.440
So how do you build something like this
and stay to those 1940s techniques?

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:25.900
We turned to what would have
been available at the time,

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:29.210
even to this silver finish
here on the helmet.

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:33.020
And in fact, the people we went to
to get this vacuum plating done,

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:34.380
they were the original company

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:37.310
that would have been doing the original
if it had ever happened.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:39.270
And in fact, when we took
the helmet into them,

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.840
they said, "Oh, well, we
finally got to do it then."

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:43.650
- There's newsreel footage from the 1940s

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:46.730
which says the BIS estimated
half a million pounds

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:48.080
to send someone to the moon.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:50.350
In today's money, that's about 20 million.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:54.730
The Apollo program roughly cost
the US government 10,000 times that.

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:58.240
It's safe to say that the
BIS's plans were optimistic.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.860
So how well would the British
spacesuit have held up

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:02.880
against an actual trip to the moon?

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.560
- We're pretty sure this
would be the Mark 1.

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:08.500
If this had ever really
walked on the moon,

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:11.480
this would have been the
Mark 8, the Mark 9.

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:13.960
Remember on the moon, you
only have to pressurise up,

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:17.900
if you're breathing pure oxygen,
to about 4½ pounds per square inch.

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:21.100
This easily would have been
capable of taking that pressure,

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:22.720
but it just would have
been pretty immobile.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:24.910
You simply can't lift
your arms up very high.

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:28.480
Because of the nature
of this deep shoulder

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:31.070
means that the arm just can't rise up.

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:34.680
- I think the BIS were very serious
about going to the moon.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:37.080
They had nowhere near
as much information

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:40.510
as NASA would have had
in the 1950s and '60s

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:42.530
about the realities of space flight,

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:45.080
and particularly the challenges
of getting to the moon.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:49.060
But certainly these weren't crazy people
just throwing around ideas.

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:51.210
There wer lots of really talented people.

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:54.940
They were just nowhere near as complex
in terms of the materials that they had,

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:56.550
but they weren't available
to them at the time.

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:57.383
So who knows?

00:05:57.383 --> 00:06:01.850
Had they have had the time and the money
to develop the sort of space-age materials

00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:05.020
that went into some of those later suits,
more time to practice

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:07.350
and actually do some practical
experiments, then who knows?

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:08.270
Quite possibly.

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:10.640
- Thank you so much to
everyone at the National Space Centre.

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:12.940
Along with the space suits,
they've got rockets and satellites,

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:14.490
and an actual piece of the moon.

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:16.990
You can find out more about them
at the link in the description.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:20.530
I mean, it's probably a good thing

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:23.180
that the real ones are made
to measure, 'cause, um...

